{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during this stay. You’re here for abdominal pain and vomiting that came back after eating tacos last night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. I was feeling great after my last hospital visit—no pain, no meds. But last night, it all came back suddenly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And you mentioned going to the ED, but they sent you home. Then your mom found you passed out in your vomit?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes. I felt awful. I didn’t pass out on purpose—I just collapsed. I was so dizzy and nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds scary. We’ll make sure you’re stable. Your vitals are okay now—blood pressure a bit low at 100/60, heart rate 95, but not alarming."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had POTS for years—my heart rate jumps when I stand. Is that related?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, POTS can cause that. We’ll keep an eye on it. You’re on betaxolol, right? That helps control your heart rate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:52:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I take it every day. But the abdominal pain is what worries me the most."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:53:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. You have a history of chronic pancreatitis. You had part of your pancreas removed a few years ago, which helped for a while."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:54:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the surgery helped a lot. But lately, the pain keeps coming back, even without drinking or eating fatty foods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:55:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve reviewed your CT scan from today. It shows no acute issues—no inflammation, blockages, or new problems in the abdomen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:56:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "But the pain is real. Why can’t they see anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:57:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I believe you. Sometimes chronic pain doesn’t show up on scans. We’ve seen this before with chronic pancreatitis—even without active inflammation, nerves can stay sensitive."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:58:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s just in my head?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2150-10-30 16:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, not at all. The pain is real. But because tests are normal, some doctors have wondered if anxiety or stress might be making it worse. That doesn’t mean it’s not physical."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:00:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do have anxiety, yes. But I don’t think that’s causing the vomiting or passing out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re right. Passing out like that needs attention. We think it could be from dehydration or a POTS flare, especially with nausea and poor intake."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. I hadn’t eaten much before the tacos."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve started IV fluids to help with that. We’re also giving you IV Dilaudid for pain and Zofran for nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The pain is already better. Thank you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2150-10-30 17:05:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We’ll keep you NPO—nothing by mouth—for now, to let your gut rest. We’ll reassess tomorrow about eating."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2150-10-31 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2150-10-31 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. Nausea is gone, and I haven’t needed pain meds since last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2150-10-31 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. We’re going to try a clear liquid diet—water, broth, apple juice. If you tolerate that, we can move to soft foods."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2150-10-31 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I have tea? I usually drink it with lemon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2150-10-31 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, weak tea without milk is fine. Start small—just a few sips. Let the nurse know if you feel any nausea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done well with liquids. We’re upgrading you to a soft diet—applesauce, toast, yogurt. How’s your energy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Still tired, but I’m not dizzy when I sit up. My face feels warm though—could that be a fever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You do have a mild facial rash and low-grade temperature. Given your Sjogren’s, this could be a mild flare. We’ll monitor it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had this before. It usually goes away with rest. Should I call my rheumatologist?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we recommend you follow up with your PCP or rheumatologist this week. We’ll continue your Plaquenil and prednisone."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the H. pylori treatment? I started it once but stopped because I was vomiting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right. You never really completed the course. We’re keeping you on omeprazole for now. Call your PCP to restart the antibiotics when you’re ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2150-10-31 12:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll do that. I just don’t want another flare like this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’ve been tolerating food well, no pain or nausea. We’d like to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Really? That’s great. But what if the pain comes back?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "If pain or vomiting returns, call your doctor or come back. We’re sending you home with acetaminophen for pain or fever."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my other meds? Should I restart everything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, resume all your home medications—Plaquenil, prednisone, betaxolol, amitriptyline, and the pancreatic enzymes. We’ve updated your list."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. I’ll follow up with my doctors and take it slow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2150-11-01 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and don’t hesitate to reach out if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2150-10-30 16:39:00",
    "admission_end": "2150-11-01 17:31:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with recurrent abdominal pain and nausea/vomiting that returned after eating tacos, following a recent hospital discharge. She reported collapsing at home in her vomit, prompting re-evaluation. CT imaging showed no acute abdominal pathology, consistent with prior unremarkable studies. Pain and nausea were managed with IV fluids, Dilaudid, and Zofran, with gradual advancement to oral intake. Her chronic pancreatitis was considered the likely contributor, though no active inflammation was seen. A mild Sjogren’s flare with facial erythema and low-grade fever was noted but remained asymptomatic. She tolerated a soft diet, had no pain, and was discharged home with instructions to resume all home medications and follow up with her PCP and rheumatologist. H. pylori treatment was deferred pending outpatient coordination.",
    "problems": [
      "Chronic pancreatitis",
      "Unspecified intestinal malabsorption",
      "Von Willebrand's disease",
      "Sicca syndrome",
      "Other specified cardiac dysrhythmias"
    ]
  }
}
